1. Start with the project overview (1 minute)
Open your Ubuntu terminal and navigate to the project:
cd ~/cn-tcp-lab

Show your teacher the README:
cat README.md

You don't need to read the entire README. Explain:
Our project compares three TCP congestion control algorithms: Reno, CUBIC and BBR. We created a controlled network environment using Mininet, with a 10 Mbps bottleneck link. We measured throughput, retransmissions, congestion window, RTT and fairness to understand how these algorithms behave under different network conditions.

2. Demonstrate your network topology (1–2 minutes)
Show the topology code:
sed -n '1,240p' topology/dumbbell.py

Explain the important components:
- Four hosts: h1, h2, h3 and h4.
- Two routers: r1 and r2.
- A shared 10 Mbps bottleneck between the routers.
- Two independent flows can travel through the same bottleneck.
Your explanation:
We use a dumbbell topology because it allows multiple TCP flows to compete for the same bottleneck. This makes it possible to observe congestion and compare how different algorithms respond to it.

You can draw this simple diagram on the board:
 h1 --------\
             r1 ===== r2 -------- h3
 h2 --------/             \------ h4
              10 Mbps
             Bottleneck

3. Demonstrate the main experiment (2 minutes)
Show the experiment code:
sed -n '1,220p' measurement/run_experiment.py

Explain your experimental parameters:
Parameter	Values
Algorithms	Reno, CUBIC, BBR
RTT	20, 50, 100 ms
Buffer size	0.5×, 1×, 2× BDP
Repetitions	3
Test duration	30 seconds


This gives:

3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81 experiments.

Tell your teacher:
We automated the experiments instead of manually running every combination. Each configuration was repeated three times to reduce the effect of random variations.

Show that the results exist:
wc -l results/tcp_matrix_results.csv

Expected output:
82 results/tcp_matrix_results.csv

That's 81 experimental records plus the header.

Important: Don't rerun the entire experiment during your demonstration. It takes time, and you've already collected the results.
4. Show your results and graphs (2 minutes)
This is probably the most important part of your demonstration.

Open the plots folder:
ls -lh plots/

Show the graphs individually:
xdg-open plots/throughput_comparison.png

xdg-open plots/retransmissions_comparison.png

xdg-open plots/throughput_buffer_20ms.png

xdg-open plots/throughput_buffer_50ms.png

xdg-open plots/throughput_buffer_100ms.png

Explain:
- Throughput measures how much useful data reaches the receiver.
- Retransmissions indicate TCP packets that needed retransmission.
- Different RTTs and buffer sizes affect how algorithms perform.
Your teacher may ask which algorithm performed best.

Don't simply say one algorithm is always best. Explain that performance depends on the network conditions and the metric being considered.
5. Demonstrate congestion window and RTT measurements (1 minute)
Show:
xdg-open plots/cwnd_vs_time.png

Then:
xdg-open plots/rtt_vs_time.png

Explain:
We also collected time-series measurements of the congestion window and round-trip time. This helps us understand how the algorithms behave over time rather than relying only on their final throughput.

Mention that this particular experiment was conducted at 100 ms RTT with an 83-packet queue.
6. Demonstrate fairness between competing flows (2 minutes)
This is another strong part of your project.

Show:
xdg-open plots/fairness_comparison.png

Then:
xdg-open plots/individual_flow_throughput.png

And:
xdg-open plots/total_throughput_fairness.png

Explain:
We introduced two simultaneous TCP flows that compete for the same bottleneck. We measured the throughput received by each flow and calculated Jain's Fairness Index.

Your actual average results:
Algorithm	Average fairness
Reno	0.9733
CUBIC	0.9383
BBR	0.9944


Explain that a fairness index closer to 1 indicates a more equal distribution of throughput.

In your tested configuration, BBR achieved the highest average fairness.

Be precise: these fairness results come from three repetitions at 100 ms RTT and a 1×BDP buffer.
7. Demonstrate packet captures (1 minute)
Show your capture files:
ls -lh captures/

Explain:
We also captured actual TCP traffic using tcpdump and analyzed the packet captures using Wireshark's command-line analyzer, TShark. This allowed us to inspect retransmissions, duplicate ACKs and TCP behavior at packet level.

You can show one capture's packet summary:
tshark -r captures/reno_test.pcap | head -15

This demonstrates that you didn't rely exclusively on the CSV results.
8. Demonstrate the Tahoe vs Reno simulator (1 minute)
Show the source:
sed -n '1,220p' simulator/tahoe_reno.py

Show its graph:
xdg-open plots/tahoe_vs_reno.png

Explain:
In addition to the experimental comparison, we created a simplified educational simulator to visualize the difference between TCP Tahoe and TCP Reno when packet loss occurs.

Make sure the simulator's actual implementation matches whatever features you claim, particularly Fast Retransmit and Fast Recovery.
